 "productionNotes": "productionTotal is null because no source consulted publishes a total for the 1970-1981 Trans Am, and the sources that come closest disagree by a handful of cars. The year-by-year figures here come from tran-zam.com, a Firebird history site whose tables credit Gary Witzenburg's history of the Firebird (its title ends in America's Premiere Performance Car), Michael Lamm's The Fabulous Firebird and John Gunnell's Standard Catalog of Firebird. Its Trans Am counts are 3,196 (1970), 2,116 (1971), 1,286 (1972), 4,802 (1973), 10,255 (1974), 27,274 (1975), 46,704 (1976), 68,744 (1977), 93,341 (1978), 117,108 (1979), 50,896 (1980) and 33,493 (1981). Added up, that is 459,215 cars, but that sum is arithmetic on this page, not a figure any source prints. The checks against it: Hagerty agrees on 1972, 1973 and 1974; Sports Car Market agrees on 1974, 1975, 1979 and 1980; Wikipedia agrees on 1977 and 1979. Three disagree. Old Cars Weekly gives 68,745 for 1977, one more than tran-zam. Wikipedia's prose says Pontiac sold 116,535 Trans Ams in 1979, against 117,108 built. And Sports Car Market prints 452,615 for 1973-1981 in one place, 451,420 for the same run less the Super Dutys in another, and 21,625 for 1970-1974; the tran-zam years sum to 452,617, 451,422 and 21,655. Variant counts are firmer. The SD-455 is 252 in 1973 (tran-zam, Wikipedia, Muscle Cars Illustrated) and 943 in 1974 (tran-zam, Fox News), 1,195 Trans Ams in all, which matches Hagerty's 1,296 SD-455 Firebirds less 101 Formulas. The 1977 Special Edition is 15,567 in three sources, split 1,861 Y81 hardtops and 13,706 Y82 T-tops. Price is thinner. No verified US list price was found for the base Trans Am in most model years; the dated figures are option prices, the $10,620 1979 Tenth Anniversary car and Sports Car Market's $7,529 1980 Turbo base price.",
